<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">States General of the Netherlands</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as States-General</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The States General of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> was the assembly of the provinces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>. It granted charters to trading companies such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a>. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-empire">Dutch Empire</a> treat it as a foundational institution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> overseas expansion.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/states-general-of-the-netherlands.jpg" alt="States General of the Netherlands" width="221" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fry1989 &amp; Sodacan · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_arms_of_the_Staten_Generaal.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The States General originated in the 15th century as an assembly of delegates from the provincial states of the Burgundian Netherlands, from about the middle of the century under the dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>. The word staten originally related to the feudal estates of clergy, nobility and commons, and came to mean the body in which they were represented. The first important session met at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruges">Bruges</a> on 9 January 1464. After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bold">Charles the Bold</a> the States General were convened on 3 February 1477 and forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-of-burgundy">Mary of Burgundy</a> to grant the Great Privilege, which recognised their right to meet on their own initiative. Their main function was to let the central government discuss matters with the provinces, especially special <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subsidy">subsidies</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1576 the States General as a whole openly rebelled against the Spanish crown, and in 1579 they split: the southern provinces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-arras">Union of Arras</a> returned to obedience, while the provinces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-utrecht">Union of Utrecht</a> continued the revolt. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/act-of-abjuration">Act of Abjuration</a> of 1581 the northern States General replaced Philip II as supreme authority. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>, a confederation, most government functions stayed with the provincial states. Each of the seven provinces had one vote, and from 1593 the States General sat continually until 1795.</p><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-utrecht">Union of Utrecht</a> they were formally sovereign, represented the Republic abroad and made treaties, and their honorific title was Hoogmogende Heren. They also supervised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-west-india-company">Dutch West India Company</a>. The southern States General were dissolved for the last time in 1634 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-iv-of-spain">Philip IV of Spain</a>. The northern body disbanded itself in January 1796. The name was revived in 1814, and after the constitutional revision of 1848 ministers became accountable to the States General, which made the king's role largely ceremonial.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Place names preserve the institution's name: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-hudson">Henry Hudson</a> called Staten Island in New York Staaten Eylandt in 1609, and Abel Tasman first named New Zealand Staten Landt. In 1815 the States General became a Senate and a House of Representatives. Universal male suffrage came in 1917 and women's suffrage in 1919. Today both chambers meet at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/binnenhof">Binnenhof</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a>, the oldest parliament building in the world still in use.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “States General of the Netherlands” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1602 the <b>States-General</b>, guided by Johan van Oldenbarnevelt, ended this wasteful competition by merging the rival Voorcompagnieën into the Vereenigde Oostindische Compagnie (VOC).</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-empire">Dutch Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The dukes held their lands by different titles under France and the Empire and tried to bind them with common institutions, a Chamber of Accounts, the <b>States General</b> from 1464 and the Order of the Golden Fleece, founded in 1430.</blockquote><a href="/state/burgundian-state">Burgundian State</a></li><li><blockquote>Regents Margaret of Austria and Mary of Hungary governed from Mechelen and Brussels with the <b>States General</b> and collateral councils.</blockquote><a href="/state/habsburg-netherlands">Habsburg Netherlands</a></li><li><blockquote>William ruled under a new constitution adopted in 1814, which gave the sovereign wide powers while keeping a <b>States General</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/sovereign-principality-of-the-united-netherlands">Sovereign Principality of the United Netherlands</a></li></ul></section>
